. Key Differences between SQL and NoSQL Databases
Feature	SQL (Relational DB)	NoSQL (Non-Relational DB)
Structure	Tables (rows & columns)	Documents, key-value, graph, column
Schema	Fixed schema	Flexible schema
Data Type	Structured data	Structured + unstructured
Scalability	Vertical (scale up)	Horizontal (scale out)
Query Language	SQL	Varies (MongoDB uses JSON-like queries)
Examples	MySQL, PostgreSQL	MongoDB, Cassandra
Example:

SQL (MySQL):

SELECT * FROM students WHERE GPA > 3.5;

NoSQL (MongoDB):

db.students.find({ GPA: { $gt: 3.5 } })
2. CAP Theorem

The CAP Theorem states that a distributed system can guarantee only two out of three properties:

Consistency (C) → All nodes see the same data
Availability (A) → Every request gets a response
Partition Tolerance (P) → System continues despite network failures
Why not all three?

In real systems, network partition (failure) is unavoidable. When it happens:

If you choose Consistency, you may sacrifice Availability
If you choose Availability, you may sacrifice Consistency

👉 So, you must choose between CP or AP systems.

3. When MongoDB is Preferred (3 Scenarios)
✅ 1. Unstructured or Semi-Structured Data
Example: Social media posts, logs
MongoDB stores flexible JSON-like documents
✅ 2. Rapid Development / Changing Schema
Example: Startups, evolving apps
No need to redesign schema frequently
✅ 3. High Scalability Requirements
Example: Big data apps, real-time analytics
MongoDB supports horizontal scaling (sharding)
4. Why MongoDB Uses BSON Instead of JSON

MongoDB internally uses BSON (Binary JSON) because:

✅ Faster processing (binary format is efficient)
✅ Supports more data types (Date, ObjectId, Binary, etc.)
✅ Efficient storage and traversal
❌ JSON is text-based → slower to parse

👉 BSON = Optimized version of JSON for performance

5. MongoDB Query

Find students with GPA > 3.5 and enrolled in CS101:

db.students.find({
  GPA: { $gt: 3.5 },
  course: "CS101"
})
If course is inside an array:
db.students.find({
  GPA: { $gt: 3.5 },
  courses: "CS101"
})
